    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p25-q5a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Use mathematical induction to prove that $n^3+3n^2+2n$ is divisible by $6$ for all $n\\in\\mathbb N$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$6\\mid(n^3+3n^2+2n)$ for all $n\\in\\mathbb N$.\n\nThe inductive step gives $6m+3(k+1)(k+2)$; the consecutive factors make the second term divisible by $6$.",
          "worked": "Base case: for $n=1$, $1^3+3\\cdot1^2+2\\cdot1=6$, which is divisible by $6$.\n\nInductive hypothesis: assume $k^3+3k^2+2k=6m$ for some integer $m$ and $k\\ge1$.\n\nInductive step:\n$$\\begin{align*}(k+1)^3+3(k+1)^2+2(k+1)&=k^3+6k^2+11k+6\\\\(k+1)^3+3(k+1)^2+2(k+1)&=(k^3+3k^2+2k)+3k^2+9k+6\\\\(k+1)^3+3(k+1)^2+2(k+1)&=6m+3(k+1)(k+2).\\end{align*}$$\nSince $k+1$ and $k+2$ are consecutive integers, their product is even. Hence $3(k+1)(k+2)$ is divisible by $6$, and so is the whole expression.\n\nBy mathematical induction, $n^3+3n^2+2n$ is divisible by $6$ for all $n\\in\\mathbb N$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 85
      },
      {
        "id": "p25-q5b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Can you justify this result without using mathematical induction?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$n^3+3n^2+2n=n(n+1)(n+2)$; the product of three consecutive integers is divisible by $6$.",
          "worked": "$$n^3+3n^2+2n=n(n+1)(n+2).$$\nAmong three consecutive integers, at least one is divisible by $2$ and one is divisible by $3$. Their product is therefore divisible by $6$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 30
      }
    ]
  },
